From: AW1000_NSS_BYTE
Subject: [PATCH] nss-drv: provide init_dummy_netdev() on kernel 6.18

The driver calls init_dummy_netdev() on the net_device it hands to napi/irq
registration:

    nss_hal/nss_hal.c:363:  init_dummy_netdev(&nss_ctx->napi_ndev);

That was an exported symbol when the driver was written. In 6.18 it is a
static helper inside net/core/dev.c with no EXPORT_SYMBOL and no prototype in
any header, so the call is an implicit declaration and the module does not
build:

    nss_hal/nss_hal.c:363:9: error: implicit declaration of function
        'init_dummy_netdev' [-Wimplicit-function-declaration]

This is not a warning and no -W flag can suppress it: there is no symbol to
link against. -Werror is irrelevant here.

Provide the equivalent locally, version-gated so older kernels keep the real
symbol. The body is the 6.18 helper verbatim (net/core/dev.c:11372): mark the
device as a dummy so it never reaches register/unregister, and mark it present
and started. Those are the only things the driver depends on - it uses the
netdev only as a napi/irq carrier and never registers it.

It has to live in nss_hal/include/nss_hal.h, not in compat.h. The driver
force-includes compat.h with -include, so it is read before
<linux/netdevice.h> defines struct net_device; putting the shim there fails
with "invalid use of undefined type 'struct net_device'" and "'NETREG_DUMMY'
undeclared". nss_hal.h includes nss_core.h, which includes <linux/netdevice.h>.

Verified by compiling the driver against 6.18 headers with
aarch64-linux-gnu-gcc: with this applied the call resolves and this is the only
error in the tree that is not a missing sibling header (qca-ssdk, qca-nss-dp,
which OpenWrt stages and a bare local build does not have).

--- a/nss_hal/include/nss_hal.h
+++ b/nss_hal/include/nss_hal.h
@@ -130,6 +130,27 @@
 
 /*
  * nss_hal_dt_parse_features()
  */
 void nss_hal_dt_parse_features(struct device_node *np, struct nss_platform_data *npd);
+/*
+ * init_dummy_netdev() stopped being exported in 6.18: net/core/dev.c now
+ * defines it static, with no EXPORT_SYMBOL and no prototype in netdevice.h.
+ * nss_hal.c calls it on the net_device that only carries napi/irq
+ * registration - it is never registered and never used as an interface, so
+ * only these three fields matter.
+ *
+ * This is a link-time break, not a diagnostic: no -W flag can affect it.
+ * It lives here rather than in compat.h because compat.h is force-included
+ * with -include, before <linux/netdevice.h> has defined struct net_device.
+ */
+#if LINUX_VERSION_CODE >= KERNEL_VERSION(6, 18, 0)
+static inline void nss_compat_init_dummy_netdev(struct net_device *dev)
+{
+	dev->reg_state = NETREG_DUMMY;
+	set_bit(__LINK_STATE_PRESENT, &dev->state);
+	set_bit(__LINK_STATE_START, &dev->state);
+}
+#define init_dummy_netdev nss_compat_init_dummy_netdev
+#endif
+
 #endif /* __NSS_HAL_H */
